{"question": "A 32-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 3-week history of intermittent loose stools and a 1.2-kg (2.6-lb) weight loss. She immigrated to the US from Uganda 6 weeks ago. Abdominal examination shows diffuse tenderness with no guarding or rebound. The liver is firm and palpable 3 cm below the right costal margin, and the spleen is palpable just below the left costal margin. Her leukocyte count is 12,800/mm3 (12% eosinophils). Stool culture shows several oval-shaped eggs with lateral spines. Microscopic examination of a liver biopsy specimen shows granulomatous inflammation with periportal fibrosis. Exposure to which of the following is most likely to have played a role in the development of this patient's symptoms?", "options": {"A": "Tsetse flies", "B": "Undercooked pork meat", "C": "Undercooked fish meat", "D": "Dog feces", "E": "Freshwater snails"}, "pred_answer": "A", "gold_answer": "E", "question_domains": ["Gastroenterology", "Infectious Disease", "Tropical Medicine", "Pathology", "Immunology"], "option_domains": ["Parasitology", "Gastroenterology"], "question_analyses": {"Gastroenterology": "The medical scenario described involves a 32-year-old woman who has recently immigrated to the US from Uganda and is experiencing intermittent loose stools and a significant weight loss over the past 3 weeks. The physical examination reveals diffuse abdominal tenderness, a firm liver palpable 3 cm below the right costal margin, and a palpable spleen below the left costal margin. Her leukocyte count is elevated, with a notable increase in eosinophils. A stool culture has identified oval-shaped eggs with lateral spines, which are characteristic of *Strongyloides stercoralis* eggs. Additionally, a liver biopsy shows granulomatous inflammation with periportal fibrosis.\n\n### Interpretation of the Condition:\nThe patient's symptoms, physical examination findings, and laboratory results strongly suggest a parasitic infection, specifically *Strongyloi